WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.699 --> 00:00:03.980
Hello everyone, my name is Erin, from IBM.

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:07.825
I'm here with you today to discuss: what is
a power systems virtual server?

00:00:07.825 --> 00:00:11.556
So, let's talk really quick about why clients, or users, in fact,

00:00:11.556 --> 00:00:15.770
might want to try out, or stay with, a power systems virtual server.

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:18.360
So, maybe they've been on-premises for a long time.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.620
Maybe they want to test out capabilities with a virtual server.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:23.956
We'll go through what those capabilities are.

00:00:23.956 --> 00:00:28.401
Historically, we find that a lot of people
like power systems because of

00:00:28.401 --> 00:00:35.489
scalability, reliability, the performance for the mission-critical workloads that it affords,

00:00:35.489 --> 00:00:39.457
as well as just that reliability. So  - and security, sorry.

00:00:39.457 --> 00:00:45.908
OK, so let's go into: what is a power systems virtual server?

00:00:45.908 --> 00:00:49.923
Well, first of all, you have your server on-premises.

00:00:49.923 --> 00:00:53.686
This is where power systems live in the data centers

00:00:53.686 --> 00:01:01.158
and they are connected to storage.

00:01:01.158 --> 00:01:05.909
Important to note here: both of these are
going to be enterprise grade,

00:01:05.909 --> 00:01:10.547
and the storage is actually going to be run on all solid-state drives.

00:01:10.547 --> 00:01:14.580
Now, what makes the virtual server portion
of this?

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:19.557
You have your operating systems that come in.

00:01:19.557 --> 00:01:21.114
Operating systems.

00:01:21.114 --> 00:01:24.712
So, the types of operating systems that you have for Power VS:

00:01:24.712 --> 00:01:33.460
AIX, IBMi, ... Linux comes to the party to hang out.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:38.867
So, these are your operating systems. IBM is going to manage up to the operating system

00:01:38.867 --> 00:01:43.634
and then the user, or the client after, can manage everything beyond that.

00:01:43.634 --> 00:01:46.387
So, you have the availability to do things like test,

00:01:46.387 --> 00:01:49.618
we'll go into some of the use cases in a bit there.

00:01:49.618 --> 00:01:53.831
What I also really like the, one of the features about this:

00:01:53.831 --> 00:02:00.980
the minimum requirement for a core on one of these operating systems is a quarter of a core.

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:02.036
That's very small.

00:02:02.036 --> 00:02:03.339
So, you don't have to get the whole server,

00:02:03.339 --> 00:02:04.617
you can get a portion of that,

00:02:04.617 --> 00:02:09.540
and you can scale as you desire with that.

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:13.268
OK, so 0.25 of a core. They can be shared or dedicated.

00:02:13.268 --> 00:02:15.022
They can be capped or uncapped.

00:02:15.022 --> 00:02:21.093
So, we'll allude to that later in the use cases, but a lot of people love
the bursting capability that that allows.

00:02:21.093 --> 00:02:25.223
Now let's go into ...

00:02:25.223 --> 00:02:28.412
I'll leave a little bit of room at the top here because,

00:02:28.412 --> 00:02:32.686
on top of your operating systems, you can run applications. Which is really cool.

00:02:32.686 --> 00:02:36.335
So, let's go by AIX first.

00:02:36.335 --> 00:02:46.474
You have your SAP HANA, Oracle and Epic.

00:02:46.474 --> 00:02:53.417
Those are applications that you can run on top of your AIX operating system.

00:02:53.417 --> 00:02:55.844
Let's go next with IBMi.

00:02:55.844 --> 00:03:02.627
So, these are a little bit different. They're going to be specific by tiers, I like to call them.

00:03:02.627 --> 00:03:09.504
So, you have your major - and those are going to be SAP, Oracle, and Infor.

00:03:09.504 --> 00:03:11.437
So, your major tiers.

00:03:11.437 --> 00:03:18.211
You have your industry tiers, which is going to be Fiserv, and Jack Henry, for example.

00:03:18.211 --> 00:03:24.788
And then you have your tools-based tier.  So, that's going to be like help systems or Precisely.

00:03:24.788 --> 00:03:31.490
And now, let's go on over to ... let's use some pink for this one ... Mr. Linux.

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:38.768
SAP HANA is what you can run, application-wise for Linux.

00:03:38.768 --> 00:03:41.426
So, we have our server connected to our storage.

00:03:41.426 --> 00:03:48.085
We have our operating systems, and we have the applications that we can run with as little as a quarter of a core.

00:03:48.085 --> 00:03:54.317
Finally, let's go over and talk about some use cases.

00:03:54.317 --> 00:03:59.178
There are quite a few, but there are 3
that I find that people really like.

00:03:59.178 --> 00:04:08.061
So, the first one, let's talk about: HA/DR - High
Availability Disaster Recovery.

00:04:08.061 --> 00:04:11.909
So, Power VS has 13 data centers globally,

00:04:11.909 --> 00:04:17.584
and then the availability to fail over disaster recovery-wise if there's a storm,

00:04:17.584 --> 00:04:21.908
if there's a, you know, natural disaster, to have another data center to connect to

00:04:21.908 --> 00:04:25.753
in the event of a disaster or just, you know, to have protection of your data.

00:04:25.753 --> 00:04:30.852
HA/DR is probably one of the top use cases that we see for Power VS.

00:04:30.852 --> 00:04:37.196
Another one I mentioned: maybe you want to test out the availability of a virtual server.

00:04:37.196 --> 00:04:41.191
What is it? What does it afford opportunity-wise?

00:04:41.191 --> 00:04:43.481
What's the future going to look like for that?

00:04:43.482 --> 00:04:45.578
A lot of people like the DevTest capability.

00:04:45.578 --> 00:04:50.363
You can, again with as little of quarter of a core, develop and test

00:04:50.363 --> 00:04:57.363
on these virtual servers with that connection into the server in the IBM data centers.

00:04:57.363 --> 00:05:01.566
So, DevTest provides a lot of people with the flexibility that they're looking for

00:05:01.566 --> 00:05:03.813
especially if you're a power on-prem customer.

00:05:03.813 --> 00:05:09.833
And then, the last one I mentioned before, the availability to burst.

00:05:09.833 --> 00:05:15.479
So, remember, with these cores, they can be shared or dedicated, capped or uncapped,

00:05:15.479 --> 00:05:17.921
and they have the availability to "burst"

00:05:17.921 --> 00:05:20.932
- as in going up and down, based on what you use.

00:05:20.932 --> 00:05:23.816
So, you can actually burst beyond the capacity of what you have

00:05:23.816 --> 00:05:26.030
if you need it, which is really nice.

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:31.710
So, Power VS is metered and billed by the hour, based on what you use.

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:35.664
So, that bursting capability is really of interest
to a lot of users.

00:05:35.664 --> 00:05:40.652
So, in a nutshell, we have our "what is a power systems virtual server" here.

00:05:40.652 --> 00:05:43.137
Thank you for being here with us today.

00:05:43.137 --> 00:05:46.233
If you have questions, please drop us a line below.

00:05:46.289 --> 00:05:50.777
If you want to see more videos like this in
the future, please like and subscribe.

